  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1036 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nTonight and Sunday...\n\nEarlier this evening, an MCS tracked across southeast South Dakota, \ngrazing extreme northeast Nebraska before gradually weakening and \nmoving east-southeast into western Iowa. The decaying complex will \nleave a shield of mid- to upper-level cloud cover across much of the \narea overnight. We could also see some patchy fog overnight in \nsouthwest Iowa where RH values will be the highest. Temperatures and \nhumidity exceeded most guidance today. Widespread highs reached the \nupper 90s to lower 100s, while dewpoints remained in the upper 70s \nto low 80s, higher than forecast by most guidance. This resulted in \nwidespread heat index values of 115 to 120 degrees. Given this \noverperformance, the entire forecast area was upgraded to an Extreme \nHeat Warning this afternoon, which will remain in effect into Sunday \nevening.\n\nSunday is shaping up to be another dangerously hot day as the region \nremains beneath a mid- to upper-level ridge covering much of the \ncentral Plains. Lingering cloud cover is expected to diminish during \nthe morning, allowing strong mixing into the warm air aloft. \nTemperatures may once again over perform guidance, with highs \nforecast in the upper 90s to low 100s and dewpoints remaining in the \nupper 70s to low 80s. Peak heat index values of 110 to 118 \ndegrees are expected. Southerly winds will also increase during \nthe afternoon, with gusts of 25 to 30 mph, though this will \nprovide little relief from the extreme heat.\n\nAnother chance for storms will arrive Sunday evening near a surface \ntrough. Strong instability will develop during the day, with MLCAPE \nvalues exceeding 2500 J/kg. However, deep-layer shear and large-\nscale forcing for ascent will remain modest, and CAM guidance \ncontinues to offer varying solutions regarding storm coverage and \nwhether initiation will occur at all in the area. Should storms\ndevelop, the greatest chance will be across northeast Nebraska \ninto western Iowa during the evening, where PoPs of 15-30% are \nin place. Activity would then gradually move southeastward and \nweaken. Damaging wind gusts will be the primary hazard, with \nDCAPE values of 1500-1800 J/kg supporting the potential for \ngusts up to 75 mph. Hail up to around 1 inch in diameter cannot \nbe ruled out initially, though the very warm near surface air \nshould limit hail from reaching the ground. Any lingering \nshowers and cloud cover will shift southward into early Monday.\n\nMonday...\n\nDangerous heat will continue into Monday, though confidence is lower \nregarding its overall extent. In addition to any lingering \nconvective debris or cloud cover, a shortwave disturbance rounding \nthe ridge across the northern Plains will push a cold front \nsouthward through the area. This will shift low-level winds from \nsoutherly to northeasterly and cut off moisture transport. The front \nwill have only a limited effect on temperatures, with highs still \nexpected in the mid 90s to low 100s, assuming residual cloud cover \nclears. However, it should lower dewpoints, particularly across \nnortheast Nebraska where the frontal passage is expected before peak \nheating. This will produce a considerable dewpoint gradient, ranging \nfrom the upper 70s across southwest Iowa to the lower 60s across \nnortheast Nebraska.\n\nPeak heat index values may reach around 115 degrees across southeast \nNebraska and southwest Iowa, decreasing to near 100 degrees across \nnortheast Nebraska. Given the lower confidence in cloud cover and \nfrontal timing, areas generally along and south of Interstate-80 \nremain split between a Heat Advisory and an Extreme Heat Watch with \nno headlines currently in place northward. A few evening storms \ncould also develop along the front across the southern portion of \nthe area, with a 15-30% chance currently in place. Model\nsoundings indicate plenty of instability, yet again, with a\nprofile supportive of strong wind gusts. \n\nTuesday and Beyond...\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave disturbance will weaken the ridge into \nthe work week, with a somewhat more comfortable post-frontal air \nmass settling into the region. High temperatures should fall into \nthe low to mid 90s for much of the work week, remaining slightly \nabove seasonal norms. \n\nAdditional shortwave disturbances rounding the ridge will bring \nperiodic precipitation chances through the period, with the greatest \npotential currently forecast Thursday into Friday, with PoPs of 40-\n60% in place.",
